{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "def get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of possible sequences of length n where each element\n    is a positive integer, greater than or equal to twice the previous element,\n    and less than or equal to m.\n    \"\"\"\n    # dp_row[j] stores the number of sequences of the current length ending with j.\n    # Initialize for sequences of length 1. Any positive integer from 1 to m can be the first element.\n    prev_dp_row = [0] * (m + 1)\n    for j in range(1, m + 1):\n        prev_dp_row[j] = 1\n\n    # Iterate for lengths from 2 up to n.\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n        current_dp_row = [0] * (m + 1)\n        \n        # Calculate prefix sums for prev_dp_row to optimize summing previous valid elements.\n        # prefix_sums[k] stores the sum of prev_dp_row[1] through prev_dp_row[k].\n        prefix_sums = [0] * (m + 1)\n        for val in range(1, m + 1):\n            prefix_sums[val] = prefix_sums[val - 1] + prev_dp_row[val]\n        \n        # Populate current_dp_row for the current length.\n        for j in range(1, m + 1): # j is the value of the current element in the sequence\n            # The previous element 'prev_j' must satisfy prev_j <= j // 2.\n            # We sum up all valid prev_dp_row[prev_j] where prev_j <= j // 2.\n            max_prev_val = j // 2\n            \n            # If max_prev_val is 0 (i.e., j=1), no positive prev_j exists, so count is 0.\n            # Otherwise, use the precomputed prefix sum.\n            if max_prev_val >= 1:\n                current_dp_row[j] = prefix_sums[max_prev_val]\n\n        # The current_dp_row becomes the prev_dp_row for the next length iteration.\n        prev_dp_row = current_dp_row\n\n    # The total number of sequences of length n is the sum of all counts in the final prev_dp_row.\n    total_sequences = sum(prev_dp_row)\n\n    return total_sequences"}
