{"task_id": "Mbpp/247", "solution": "def lps(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the length of the longest palindromic subsequence in the given string.\n    Uses dynamic programming to fill a table storing LPS lengths for all substrings.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n\n    # dp[i][j] stores the length of the longest palindromic subsequence of s[i...j]\n    # Initialize all entries to 0.\n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n\n    # All single characters are palindromic subsequences of length 1.\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[i][i] = 1\n\n    # Fill the table for substring lengths from 2 to n.\n    # `length` represents the current substring length.\n    for length in range(2, n + 1):\n        # `i` is the starting index of the substring.\n        for i in range(n - length + 1):\n            # `j` is the ending index of the substring.\n            j = i + length - 1\n\n            # If the characters at the ends of the current substring match.\n            if s[i] == s[j]:\n                # They contribute 2 to the LPS length, plus the LPS of the inner substring s[i+1...j-1].\n                # dp[i+1][j-1] will correctly be 0 if i+1 > j-1 (e.g., for length 2 substrings like \"aa\").\n                dp[i][j] = 2 + dp[i + 1][j - 1]\n            else:\n                # If characters don't match, the LPS is the maximum of:\n                # 1. LPS of s[i+1...j] (excluding s[i])\n                # 2. LPS of s[i...j-1] (excluding s[j])\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i + 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])\n\n    # The result for the entire string s is stored in dp[0][n-1].\n    return dp[0][n - 1]"}
