{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(value: int) -> int:\n    # Finds the position of the least significant unset bit (0) in the integer.\n    # It then sets that bit to 1 and returns the modified integer.\n    i = 0\n    while True:\n        if ((value >> i) & 1) == 0:  # Check if the i-th bit is 0\n            return value | (1 << i) # Set the i-th bit to 1\n        i += 1 # Move to the next bit position"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/308", "solution": "def large_product(list1: list[float], list2: list[float], k: int) -> list[float]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the specified number of largest products from two given lists,\n    selecting one factor from each list.\n    \"\"\"\n    class Product:\n        \"\"\"Encapsulates a product value and its two factors, enabling comparison for sorting.\"\"\"\n        def __init__(self, factor1: float, factor2: float):\n            self.factor1 = factor1\n            self.factor2 = factor2\n            self.value = factor1 * factor2\n\n        def __lt__(self, other):\n            \"\"\"Compares two Product objects based on their 'value' for sorting purposes.\"\"\"\n            return self.value < other.value\n\n    all_products_objects = []\n\n    # Compute all possible products and create Product objects.\n    for num1 in list1:\n        for num2 in list2:\n            all_products_objects.append(Product(num1, num2))\n\n    # Sort the products in descending order based on their value.\n    all_products_objects.sort(reverse=True)\n\n    # Extract the values of the top k largest products.\n    return [p.value for p in all_products_objects[:k]]"}
