{"task_id": "Mbpp/306", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef max_sum_increasing_subseq(arr: List[int], size: int, i: int, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence from a prefix `0..i`\n    that includes `array[k]` and where prefix elements are less than `array[k]`.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Input Validation: Ensure indices are within bounds and k is strictly greater than i.\n    if not (0 <= i < size and 0 <= k < size and k > i):\n        return 0\n\n    k_element_value = arr[k]\n\n    # dp_prefix_sums[j] stores the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence\n    # ending at index j (where j <= i), such that arr[j] is less than k_element_value.\n    dp_prefix_sums = [0] * (i + 1)\n\n    # max_sum_for_prefix tracks the maximum sum found among all valid prefix subsequences\n    # that can precede arr[k]. It is initialized to 0 to handle cases where no such prefix\n    # exists or only negative sums are possible from the prefix elements.\n    max_sum_for_prefix = 0\n\n    # Iterate through the prefix elements from index 0 up to i (inclusive).\n    for j in range(i + 1):\n        # Only consider elements arr[j] that are strictly less than arr[k]\n        # to ensure the overall increasing subsequence property with arr[k].\n        if arr[j] < k_element_value:\n            # Initialize the maximum sum ending at j with arr[j] itself (a subsequence of length 1).\n            current_max_ending_at_j = arr[j]\n\n            # Look for previous elements p (from 0 to j-1) that can extend the subsequence.\n            for p in range(j):\n                # If arr[p] is less than arr[j] (maintaining increasing order)\n                # and dp_prefix_sums[p] holds a valid positive sum for a subsequence ending at p,\n                # then consider extending that subsequence with arr[j].\n                if arr[p] < arr[j] and dp_prefix_sums[p] > 0:\n                    current_max_ending_at_j = max(current_max_ending_at_j, arr[j] + dp_prefix_sums[p])\n                # Note: If dp_prefix_sums[p] is 0 or negative, it means extending that path\n                # might not be beneficial. By initializing current_max_ending_at_j with arr[j]\n                # and comparing, we implicitly choose to start a new subsequence at arr[j]\n                # if extending an older one (potentially with negative dp_prefix_sums[p])\n                # results in a smaller sum.\n\n            # Store the computed maximum sum ending at index j.\n            dp_prefix_sums[j] = current_max_ending_at_j\n            \n            # Update the overall maximum sum found across all prefix elements.\n            max_sum_for_prefix = max(max_sum_for_prefix, dp_prefix_sums[j])\n\n    # The final result is the maximum sum found from the prefix part plus the mandatory\n    # value of the element at index k. If max_sum_for_prefix is 0, it means no valid\n    # prefix contribution was found, and the sum will simply be arr[k].\n    return max_sum_for_prefix + k_element_value"}
