{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "def find_kth(arr1, arr2, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the kth smallest element from two sorted arrays using a binary search approach.\n    \"\"\"\n    m, n = len(arr1), len(arr2)\n\n    # Ensure arr1 is the smaller or equal length array for optimization.\n    if m > n:\n        arr1, arr2 = arr2, arr1\n        m, n = n, m\n\n    # Define the binary search range for partition1 in arr1.\n    # partition1 must be at least max(0, k - n) (if k > n, at least k-n elements must come from arr1).\n    # partition1 must be at most min(k, m) (cannot take more than k elements total, or more than m from arr1).\n    low, high = max(0, k - n), min(k, m)\n\n    while low <= high:\n        # partition1 is the number of elements taken from arr1.\n        partition1 = (low + high) // 2\n        # partition2 is the number of elements taken from arr2 to reach total k.\n        partition2 = k - partition1\n\n        # Determine boundary elements for arr1.\n        # If partition is 0, there's no element to its left, so max_left is -inf.\n        # If partition is len(arr1), there's no element to its right, so min_right is +inf.\n        max_left1 = arr1[partition1 - 1] if partition1 > 0 else float('-inf')\n        min_right1 = arr1[partition1] if partition1 < m else float('inf')\n\n        # Determine boundary elements for arr2.\n        max_left2 = arr2[partition2 - 1] if partition2 > 0 else float('-inf')\n        min_right2 = arr2[partition2] if partition2 < n else float('inf')\n\n        # Check if the partitions are valid.\n        # A valid partition means that max element on the left side of either array\n        # is less than or equal to the min element on the right side of the other array.\n        if max_left1 <= min_right2 and max_left2 <= min_right1:\n            # If valid, the kth element is the maximum of the elements at the partition boundary on the left side.\n            return max(max_left1, max_left2)\n        elif max_left1 > min_right2:\n            # If max_left1 is too large, it means we took too many elements from arr1; move partition1 left.\n            high = partition1 - 1\n        else:\n            # If max_left2 is too large (or max_left1 is too small), it means we took too few elements from arr1; move partition1 right.\n            low = partition1 + 1"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/603", "solution": "def get_ludic(n: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates and returns a sorted list of lucid numbers up to a given integer n.\n    Lucid numbers are defined as 1 and all prime numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def is_prime(num: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Determines if a given integer is a prime number.\n        Handles edge cases for numbers less than 2.\n        \"\"\"\n        if num < 2:\n            return False\n        for i in range(2, int(num**0.5) + 1):\n            if num % i == 0:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    ludic_numbers = []\n\n    if n >= 1:\n        ludic_numbers.append(1)\n\n    for x in range(2, n + 1):\n        if is_prime(x):\n            ludic_numbers.append(x)\n\n    # The list is inherently sorted because numbers are added in increasing order.\n    return ludic_numbers"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/604", "solution": "def reverse_words(input_string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Reverses the order of words in a string, handling multiple spaces and trimming leading/trailing spaces.\n    Words are separated by single spaces in the output.\n    \"\"\"\n    words = input_string.strip().split(' ')\n    filtered_words = [word for word in words if word]\n    filtered_words.reverse()\n    return ' '.join(filtered_words)"}
