{"task_id": "Mbpp/618", "solution": "def div_list(list_a: list[float | int], list_b: list[float | int]) -> list[float]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Divides two lists element-wise, returning a new list of floating-point results.\n    Raises ValueError if lists have different lengths or ZeroDivisionError for division by zero.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(list_a) != len(list_b):\n        raise ValueError(\"Lists must have equal length\")\n\n    results = []\n    for i in range(len(list_a)):\n        numerator = list_a[i]\n        denominator = list_b[i]\n\n        if denominator == 0:\n            raise ZeroDivisionError(\"Cannot divide by zero\")\n\n        results.append(float(numerator) / denominator)\n\n    return results"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/620", "solution": "def largest_subset(numbers: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the size of the largest subset of numbers where every pair (a, b) satisfies (a % b == 0 or b % a == 0).\n    Uses dynamic programming after sorting the input list.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(numbers)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # Sort the numbers to simplify divisibility checks: if a divides b and b divides c, then a divides c.\n    numbers.sort()\n\n    # dp[i] will store the size of the largest divisible subset ending with numbers[i].\n    dp = [1] * n\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            # If numbers[i] is divisible by numbers[j], it can extend the subset ending at numbers[j].\n            if numbers[i] % numbers[j] == 0:\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)\n\n    # The maximum value in dp array is the size of the largest divisible subset.\n    return max(dp)"}
